  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow:\n\nWater vapor imagery continues to feature a mid/upper shortwave \nriding the northern side of zonal flow set up along the US/Canadian \nborder while another shortwave ejects northeastward into the Four \nCorners Region. Ahead of those features, a large-scale mass response \nis underway with south-southwesterly winds up and down the Great \nPlains, with those southerly winds helping to rebound \ntemperatures from the 40s this morning well into the 50s on \ntheir way to their highs in the mid-to-upper 60s. Much of that \nflow is sourced from the High Plains into central Texas, leaving\nlittle moisture for the incoming southwestern wave to work \nwith. We'll see increasing cloud cover moving in this evening, \nlasting through much of the day tomorrow as highs top out in the\nmid 60s. Winds will decrease in speed heading into tomorrow as \nwell, as the surface pressure gradient decreases and the \nincoming front to the northwest stalls out before arriving.\n\nTuesday and Beyond:\n\nWe'll see temperatures warm through the remainder of the week as \nmid/upper ridging increases in amplitude, propped up be a deepening \ntrough off the Pacific Coast. Highs will top out Thursday/Friday in \nthe mid 70s, with increasing flow once again in anticipation of\nthe main system associated with the incoming trough. EPS \nmembers give northeast Nebraska greater than a 70% chance to see\ndry conditions alongside winds of 35 mph or greater. Those \nsouthern winds will be bringing in better moisture compared to \nthe early week, meaning humidity values won't be critically \nlow. With that said, area land managers and fire experts have \nobserved extreme fire behavior with strong winds of 40 mph or \ngreater with RH values of 50% or less. The best chances of that \nhappening will be across our driest areas of northeast Nebraska,\nwhere there are still plenty of standing, fully cured crops \nwaiting to be harvested. We'll continue to monitor the forecast \ntrends, but very high to extreme fire danger is on the table \nFriday afternoon. Rain chances arrive Saturday into Sunday, with\nthe main core of the upper system shooting very quickly to the \nnorth. This will decrease the forcing locally and limit the \nmagnitude of the anticipated rainfall. Either way, we'll take \nit so we doing dry out too much during the peaking fall fire \nseason.",
